{"causes": ["The collapse of the Akkadian Empire created a power vacuum in Mesopotamia that allowed foreign groups like the Gutians to establish control.", "The successful invasion and occupation of Sumer by the Gutian tribes established a new dynastic line that included Yarlagab.", "The later compilation of the Sumerian King List by scribes aimed to legitimize kingship by providing a continuous historical narrative, including Gutian rulers."], "effects": ["Yarlagab's reign contributed to the continuation of Gutian rule over parts of Sumer during a period of political fragmentation.", "His place in the Sumerian King List ensured his name was preserved as a legitimate successor in the ancient Mesopotamian concept of kingship.", "Yarlagab's succession by Ibate furthered the chronological sequence of the Gutian Dynasty as recorded in historical tradition."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "sumerian-king-list", "sourceName": "Sumerian King List", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "yarlagab", "targetName": "Yarlagab", "context": "The Sumerian King List formally includes Yarlagab as the 7th Gutian ruler, thereby legitimizing his place in the historical lineage of kingship."}, {"sourceSlug": "yarlagab", "sourceName": "Yarlagab", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "igeshaush", "targetName": "Igeshaush", "context": "According to the Sumerian King List, Yarlagab directly followed Igeshaush as the ruler of the Gutian Dynasty."}, {"sourceSlug": "ibate", "sourceName": "Ibate", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "yarlagab", "targetName": "Yarlagab", "context": "Ibate became the eighth Gutian king, succeeding Yarlagab according to the established order of the Sumerian King List."}, {"sourceSlug": "yarlagab", "sourceName": "Yarlagab", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ancient-mesopotamia", "targetName": "Ancient Mesopotamia", "context": "Yarlagab's reign as a Gutian king took place within the geographical and cultural sphere of ancient Mesopotamia."}, {"sourceSlug": "gutian-dynasty", "sourceName": "Gutian Dynasty", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "yarlagab", "targetName": "Yarlagab", "context": "Yarlagab was a named monarch within the sequence of rulers comprising the Gutian Dynasty, which dominated Sumer for approximately 100 years."}], "places": [{"name": "Sumer, Ancient Mesopotamia", "role": "Seat of Power"}, {"name": "Akkad, Ancient Mesopotamia", "role": "Predecessor Empire"}, {"name": "Zagros Mountains, Ancient Mesopotamia", "role": "Gutian Homeland"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Yarlagab", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "yarlagab"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1977196", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarlagab"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 4, "significanceNarrative": "Yarlagab's significance lies in his role as a named ruler within the Gutian Dynasty, representing a period of political fragmentation and foreign domination after the Akkadian Empire, as recorded in the Sumerian King List.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-19T08:54:09Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29680535373", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Yarlagab was the 7th Gutian ruler of the Gutian Dynasty of Sumer mentioned on the "Sumerian King List" (SKL). According to the SKL: Yarlagab was the successor of Igeshaush. Ibate then succeeded Yarlagab, likewise according to the SKL.", "newValue": "Yarlagab, designated as the seventh ruler of the Gutian Dynasty of Sumer, holds a place in the ancient Near Eastern historical record primarily through his mention on the "Sumerian King List" (SKL). His reign, though undated precisely, falls within the Gutian period, a tumultuous interval in Mesopotamian history following the collapse of the Akkadian Empire (c. 2154 BCE) and preceding the rise of the Neo-Sumerian Ur III Dynasty (c. 2112 BCE). The Gutians, originating from the Zagros Mountains, are often depicted in later Sumerian literature as a disruptive, barbarian force, and Yarlagab's inclusion in the SKL underscores their temporary but significant control over much of Sumer.

According to the SKL, the primary source for his existence, Yarlagab succeeded Igeshaush, who was the sixth Gutian king. His own rule was then followed by that of Ibate, the eighth Gutian monarch. While the SKL attributes brief reigns to many Gutian kings, often just a few years, it provides no further details on Yarlagab's specific achievements, policies, or the duration of his time on the throne. The Gutian period as a whole is characterized by a fragmented political landscape, a decline in centralized authority, and a relative scarcity of monumental inscriptions compared to the preceding Akkadian era.

Yarlagab's legacy is thus intertwined with the broader narrative of the Gutian interregnum: a period remembered more for what it wasn't (the centralized might of Akkad or the cultural renaissance of Ur III) than for its own internal developments. The Gutian Dynasty represented a temporary eclipse of Sumerian political autonomy, paving the way for the eventual resurgence of Sumerian power under rulers like Ur-Namma of Ur. His name, alongside other Gutian kings, serves as a chronological marker within the long sweep of Mesopotamian history, demonstrating the cyclical nature of imperial rise and fall."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-19T08:54:09Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29680535373", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The collapse of the Akkadian Empire created a power vacuum in Mesopotamia that allowed foreign groups like the Gutians to establish control.", "The successful invasion and occupation of Sumer by the Gutian tribes established a new dynastic line that included Yarlagab.", "The later compilation of the Sumerian King List by scribes aimed to legitimize kingship by providing a continuous historical narrative, including Gutian rulers."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-19T08:54:09Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29680535373", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical"}], "newValue": ["Yarlagab's reign contributed to the continuation of Gutian rule over parts of Sumer during a period of political fragmentation.", "His place in the Sume